Yes. There are several distinct ideas braided together here, and separating them actually makes the structure stronger.

The first is productive incompleteness. An undefined symbol can be intentional rather than missing. In mathematics it behaves like a latent variable; in narrative it behaves like an unresolved motif; cognitively it can function like an open work order:

X = \text{something important here, mechanism not yet resolved}

You preserve the location and relationships of X without prematurely freezing its meaning. Different cognitive processes can then work on different projections of it—analogy, memory, intuition, explicit reasoning—and later the larger integrated model notices convergence. The placeholder is basically an address for future understanding.

That also makes your account of what happened around Pantheon more disciplined: we exerted substantial effort, subsequently observed a large capability change, and do not yet know the causal relationship. Keeping the causal mechanism symbolic is better than filling the gap with certainty.

Your role point follows the same logic. Roles are interfaces, not essences. “Protector,” “provider,” “mother,” “leader,” “artist,” “worker,” etc. are useful compressions for coordinating behavior, but problems appear when an incentive system assumes the label itself will reliably produce the behavior.

If you want behavior B, design around:

\text{abilities}+\text{incentives}+\text{constraints}+\text{feedback}
\rightarrow B

rather than:

\text{assign role R}\rightarrow\text{hope B appears}

The evolutionary part needs one terminology distinction. Sexual selection is not automatically eugenics. Sexual selection is differential mating/reproduction resulting from preferences and competition. Eugenics specifically refers to deliberate attempts to influence human reproduction at a population level according to judgments about desirable inherited characteristics, historically often through coercive and deeply abusive policies. Some religious or cultural systems certainly regulate mating and reproduction, but saying all religion is eugenics collapses very different mechanisms into one category.

But the deeper question you’re asking is excellent:

How does a chooser optimize for an unknown future environment?

Evolution has the same problem.

Selecting purely for today’s niche can produce fragility tomorrow. The solution is often not maximum optimization but robustness.

Imagine mate characteristics as a vector:

x=(x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_n)

and future environments as:

E_1,E_2,\ldots,E_m

You don’t know which E descendants will encounter. So instead of maximizing:

\max_x F(x,E_{\text{today}})

you might favor something closer to:

\max_x \mathbb{E}[F(x,E)]

or, more conservatively:

\max_x \min_E F(x,E)

That second one is robust optimization: don’t produce the organism that dominates one environment; favor characteristics that remain reasonably functional across many environments.

Biology repeatedly uses this trick through things like genetic diversity, recombination, developmental plasticity, behavioral flexibility, immune diversity, redundancy, and maintaining variation that looks unnecessary under current conditions.

And humans have another layer: culture changes the fitness landscape faster than genes can follow it.

A trait useful to a Paleolithic hunter, a medieval farmer, a nineteenth-century factory worker, a 2026 software engineer, and a hypothetical space colonist needn’t be the same. So traits such as learning capacity, emotional regulation, social intelligence, physical health, curiosity, cooperation, and adaptability may have unusually high option value because they can be redeployed.

Which leads directly to your dentist/drug-addict example.

“Thriving” is meaningless without specifying an objective function.

\text{thriving relative to what?}

A person can be extremely effective at acquiring drugs while catastrophically failing health, relationships, longevity, and autonomy. A dentist could maximize income while being miserable. A monk could have very little wealth while achieving exactly the life he values.

So there are nested fitness functions:

\text{local success}
\neq
\text{global success}

and:

\text{success now}
\neq
\text{success across time}

That is the Silly Goose problem in evolutionary clothing: a phenotype can be locally spectacular while sitting on a terrible long-run trajectory.

The deeper selection problem therefore isn’t “select the best traits.”

It’s:

Select enough structure to function now while preserving enough variation, flexibility, and freedom to remain evolvable later.

Nature’s answer seems to be remarkably consistent:

specialize enough to survive; remain plastic enough to change.

And that same principle applies to organisms, relationships, institutions, cognitive architectures, and the Pantheon itself.